     "tests_reduce_only_clamp": "that the venue always CLAMPS a reduceOnly order that exceeds the position rather than refusing it — observed on 2026-09-12 12Z (MEMEUSDT/POPCATUSDT, origQty = the position to the contract, no error) and documented, assumed general; and that the allOrders record of a clamped request has the shape this suite rebuilds from the submit response + the child fills (the live record was not logged). It proves, against the real gates (`submit_identity_mismatch`, `last_fill_details`, `venue_fills.settle_ambiguous_legs`) driven by the REAL 12Z submit responses and the REAL 08Z/12Z readbacks, that a reduceOnly record whose origQty is BELOW ours is the fourth state `clamped` (recorded, orig_qty = the position, no `inconsistent`), that an origQty ABOVE ours, a non-reduceOnly difference and a wrong side are still contradictions at every gate (the 1e-6 tolerance is untouched), that `plan()` sizes a full exit as −held_qty from the same account snapshot (F2 removed at the source; no quantity ⇒ the old path, labelled `notional_over_mid`), and end to end through the unmodified plan→submit_maker→complete_anchor chain over a scripted transport that the maker rows close on the venue's capacity (`qty_venue`) and `reconcile` measures both names at residual 0 where the old code produced 2 × execution_of_unknown_size (the incident, reproduced from the live rows as a control; the origQty-above-ours mutation of the same chain still produces the 2 anomalies). It does NOT run the watchdog (the proportional response is (c), a separate ruling), nor prove that mark ≠ mid is the ONLY way ours can exceed the position.",
+    "tests_unknown_size_local_response": "that a LOCAL response is the right response — that is the user's ruling, and this suite only makes the ruling a one-line flip with both sides tested. It proves, on the real 2026-09-12 12Z rows laid out as a pilot_log day, that `watchdog.UNKNOWN_SIZE_LOCAL_RESPONSE` defaults to False and that with it False the incident's response is unchanged (§4-5b + §4-7 trip, the ladder's flatten_all, `degradation` in state) while reconcile RECORDS the eligibility (2 names, 0.65% of gross, venue records self-consistent); that with it True the same rows halt opening + write a HIGH alert + re-check those two names reduce-only and flatten NOTHING (no trigger, §4-7 reads the same partition, state kind local_response with tripped_at so the anchor re-applies the halt), that the re-check flattens only the unexplained part of a NAMED name above the dust floor and writes its rows, and that 6 names / 3% of gross / a readback the known part does not explain / child fills that do not add up / another anomaly kind / no anchors row / an unrelated trigger all still run the full ladder with the switch on. It does NOT prove the venue re-read at response time is fresher than the readback the anomaly was judged on (MockBroker), nor that the off-box page fires (the anchor reads `tripped_at`, unchanged), nor that 2% / 5 names are the right numbers (policy, frozen before data).",
     "tests_income_twin_rows": "that the venue keys income rows the way the fixture does — it proves, against an in-process fake of /fapi/v1/income, that a trade's COMMISSION and REALIZED_PNL twins (same tranId) are BOTH summed, that a row re-served across a page boundary is still counted once, and that the old tranId-only key would have dropped exactly one twin per trade (positive control). Whether the venue also shares tranIds across OTHER pairs of types, or emits a third row per trade in some product, is a fact about the venue that only the live ledger reconciliation (income vs fills) can keep proving.",
     "tests_transport_resilience": "that the VENUE will be reachable on the resend — it proves, against an in-process fake of urlopen, that a request-phase failure on GET/DELETE is resent a bounded number of times while an order POST is NEVER resent under any failure — transport in either phase, HTTP 5xx, 200 non-JSON — but settled only by the venue's own order record (found ⇒ adopted, -2013 twice ⇒ recorded as not sent, else ambiguous), that the requote carries ambiguity as rested and the top-up keeps a confirmed chunk and writes UNKNOWN never zero, that a still-unresolvable POST is carried as possibly-resting, that the executor records transport rows and trips its circuit breaker, and that an exception escaping the submit loop cancels what rested, writes rows and pages before re-raising. It cannot see whether the venue actually processed a request whose answer we lost when the query ALSO fails (that order is handed to phase B as possibly-resting and to the next sweep), nor whether a 0.5 s pause is long enough for the venue to have decided before we ask it, nor whether Telegram delivers the page",
     "tests_stale_order_sweep": "that the VENUE lists an order we left behind — the whole sweep rests on openOrders returning it. If the venue omitted a resting order, or if a fill landed between the query and the cancel, this would look identical and the stale order would survive. It closes the gap between anchors, not the gap inside one",
